import{_ as e,o as i,c as d,an as a}from"./chunks/framework.gfCnttQ-.js";const y=JSON.parse('{"title":"Nonlinear weighting evidence","description":"","frontmatter":{},"headers":[],"relativePath":"nonlinear-weighting.md","filePath":"nonlinear-weighting.md","lastUpdated":null}'),r={name:"nonlinear-weighting.md"};function n(l,t,o,s,h,g){return i(),d("div",null,[...t[0]||(t[0]=[a('<h1 id="Nonlinear-weighting-evidence" tabindex="-1">Nonlinear weighting evidence <a class="header-anchor" href="#Nonlinear-weighting-evidence" aria-label="Permalink to &quot;Nonlinear weighting evidence {#Nonlinear-weighting-evidence}&quot;">​</a></h1><p>The adaptive nonlinear path collects evidence from the leaves of each trajectory. Two independent choices govern that: <strong>which leaves contribute</strong>, and <strong>how their contribution is scaled</strong>. Both are exposed as keyword arguments, and this page is the measured answer to whether the second one matters.</p><p>Every figure below is generated at docs-build time from <code>docs/benchmark/results/nonlinear_weighting/rows.json</code>, which holds one object per run plus the configuration that produced them — and no stored summaries or conclusion. The summaries, the conclusion included, are computed by <code>docs/benchmark/nonlinear_weighting.jl</code>, the same derivation the report uses; the web app reads the same JSON through its own generic evidence renderer. Nothing on this page is a second copy of a number, and if the rows go missing the docs build fails rather than rendering a stale table.</p><h2 id="What-was-varied" tabindex="-1">What was varied <a class="header-anchor" href="#What-was-varied" aria-label="Permalink to &quot;What was varied {#What-was-varied}&quot;">​</a></h2><table tabindex="0"><thead><tr><th style="text-align:right;">axis</th><th style="text-align:right;">keyword</th><th style="text-align:right;">arms measured</th><th style="text-align:right;">shipped default</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td style="text-align:right;">which leaves contribute</td><td style="text-align:right;"><code>nonlinear_evidence</code></td><td style="text-align:right;"><code>:all_good_leaves</code>, <code>:nuts_weighted</code></td><td style="text-align:right;"><code>:linear_pool</code></td></tr><tr><td style="text-align:right;">how they are scaled</td><td style="text-align:right;"><code>nonlinear_trajectory_weighting</code></td><td style="text-align:right;"><code>:unit</code>, <code>:stepsize</code></td><td style="text-align:right;"><code>:unit</code></td></tr></tbody></table><div class="warning custom-block"><p class="custom-block-title">Neither leaf-policy arm is the default</p><p><code>nonlinear_evidence</code> ships as <code>:linear_pool</code>, and the study did not measure it. The leaf-policy axis therefore compares two <strong>opt-in</strong> modes against each other, not against what you get by default. Read the rows below as &quot;given that you have already opted into one of these two, does the scaling matter&quot;, not as a verdict on the shipped configuration.</p></div><p>The scaling axis is different: <code>:unit</code> <strong>is</strong> the default, so the <code>unit</code>-vs-<code>stepsize</code> comparison does speak directly to a choice you already have.</p><h2 id="Can-the-target-tell-the-arms-apart?" tabindex="-1">Can the target tell the arms apart? <a class="header-anchor" href="#Can-the-target-tell-the-arms-apart?" aria-label="Permalink to &quot;Can the target tell the arms apart? {#Can-the-target-tell-the-arms-apart?}&quot;">​</a></h2><p>This is the gate, and it belongs before any efficiency number. If two policy arms produce a bit-identical run on a target, then every comparison on that target is a tie by construction — and a tie reported without this check reads as evidence that the policies are equivalent, which it is not.</p><table tabindex="0"><thead><tr><th style="text-align:right;">target</th><th style="text-align:right;">seeds</th><th style="text-align:right;">all arms identical</th><th style="text-align:right;">centering differs</th><th style="text-align:right;">verdict</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td style="text-align:right;"><code>eight_schools</code></td><td style="text-align:right;">20</td><td style="text-align:right;">12 / 20</td><td style="text-align:right;">8 / 20</td><td style="text-align:right;">discriminates on 8/20 seeds</td></tr><tr><td style="text-align:right;"><code>funnel</code></td><td style="text-align:right;">20</td><td style="text-align:right;">20 / 20</td><td style="text-align:right;">0 / 20</td><td style="text-align:right;">cannot discriminate — every arm identical</td></tr><tr><td style="text-align:right;"><code>radon_mn-radon_partially_pooled_centered</code></td><td style="text-align:right;">20</td><td style="text-align:right;">0 / 20</td><td style="text-align:right;">20 / 20</td><td style="text-align:right;">discriminates on 20/20 seeds</td></tr></tbody></table><p>Read that column-by-column before trusting anything downstream. A target where every arm is identical contributes ties that measure the target, not the policy.</p><h2 id="Efficiency-per-cell" tabindex="-1">Efficiency per cell <a class="header-anchor" href="#Efficiency-per-cell" aria-label="Permalink to &quot;Efficiency per cell {#Efficiency-per-cell}&quot;">​</a></h2><p>Minimum-coordinate ESS per thousand gradient evaluations — the portable measure; wall time is host-specific and is not reported here.</p><table tabindex="0"><thead><tr><th style="text-align:right;">target</th><th style="text-align:right;">leaves</th><th style="text-align:right;">metric</th><th style="text-align:right;">min ESS/kgrad</th><th style="text-align:right;">median ESS/kgrad</th><th style="text-align:right;">divergences</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td style="text-align:right;"><code>eight_schools</code></td><td style="text-align:right;"><code>all_good_leaves</code></td><td style="text-align:right;"><code>stepsize</code></td><td style="text-align:right;">47.53</td><td style="text-align:right;">105.53</td><td style="text-align:right;">6</td></tr><tr><td style="text-align:right;"><code>eight_schools</code></td><td style="text-align:right;"><code>all_good_leaves</code></td><td style="text-align:right;"><code>unit</code></td><td style="text-align:right;">47.53</td><td style="text-align:right;">104.13</td><td style="text-align:right;">4</td></tr><tr><td style="text-align:right;"><code>eight_schools</code></td><td style="text-align:right;"><code>nuts_weighted</code></td><td style="text-align:right;"><code>stepsize</code></td><td style="text-align:right;">47.20</td><td style="text-align:right;">107.35</td><td style="text-align:right;">5</td></tr><tr><td style="text-align:right;"><code>eight_schools</code></td><td style="text-align:right;"><code>nuts_weighted</code></td><td style="text-align:right;"><code>unit</code></td><td style="text-align:right;">43.48</td><td style="text-align:right;">101.90</td><td style="text-align:right;">4</td></tr><tr><td style="text-align:right;"><code>funnel</code></td><td style="text-align:right;"><code>all_good_leaves</code></td><td style="text-align:right;"><code>stepsize</code></td><td style="text-align:right;">101.93</td><td style="text-align:right;">121.19</td><td style="text-align:right;">91</td></tr><tr><td style="text-align:right;"><code>funnel</code></td><td style="text-align:right;"><code>all_good_leaves</code></td><td style="text-align:right;"><code>unit</code></td><td style="text-align:right;">101.93</td><td style="text-align:right;">121.19</td><td style="text-align:right;">91</td></tr><tr><td style="text-align:right;"><code>funnel</code></td><td style="text-align:right;"><code>nuts_weighted</code></td><td style="text-align:right;"><code>stepsize</code></td><td style="text-align:right;">101.93</td><td style="text-align:right;">121.19</td><td style="text-align:right;">91</td></tr><tr><td style="text-align:right;"><code>funnel</code></td><td style="text-align:right;"><code>nuts_weighted</code></td><td style="text-align:right;"><code>unit</code></td><td style="text-align:right;">101.93</td><td style="text-align:right;">121.19</td><td style="text-align:right;">91</td></tr><tr><td style="text-align:right;"><code>radon_mn-radon_partially_pooled_centered</code></td><td style="text-align:right;"><code>all_good_leaves</code></td><td style="text-align:right;"><code>stepsize</code></td><td style="text-align:right;">61.58</td><td style="text-align:right;">110.35</td><td style="text-align:right;">0</td></tr><tr><td style="text-align:right;"><code>radon_mn-radon_partially_pooled_centered</code></td><td style="text-align:right;"><code>all_good_leaves</code></td><td style="text-align:right;"><code>unit</code></td><td style="text-align:right;">65.72</td><td style="text-align:right;">120.52</td><td style="text-align:right;">0</td></tr><tr><td style="text-align:right;"><code>radon_mn-radon_partially_pooled_centered</code></td><td style="text-align:right;"><code>nuts_weighted</code></td><td style="text-align:right;"><code>stepsize</code></td><td style="text-align:right;">60.45</td><td style="text-align:right;">109.90</td><td style="text-align:right;">0</td></tr><tr><td style="text-align:right;"><code>radon_mn-radon_partially_pooled_centered</code></td><td style="text-align:right;"><code>nuts_weighted</code></td><td style="text-align:right;"><code>unit</code></td><td style="text-align:right;">69.97</td><td style="text-align:right;">111.74</td><td style="text-align:right;">0</td></tr></tbody></table><p><em>Measured on WarmupHMC <code>ef3a6af</code>, Julia 1.10.11, 1 BLAS thread, by <code>docs/benchmark/nonlinear_weighting_run.jl</code>. 240 runs; summaries derived by <code>docs/benchmark/nonlinear_weighting.jl</code> at build time.</em></p><h2 id="Paired-comparison" tabindex="-1">Paired comparison <a class="header-anchor" href="#Paired-comparison" aria-label="Permalink to &quot;Paired comparison {#Paired-comparison}&quot;">​</a></h2><p>Seeds are paired, so each ratio compares <code>stepsize</code> against <code>unit</code> on the <em>same</em> seed. Above 1 favours <code>stepsize</code>. The five-number summary is here for the reason a median alone is misleading on this data: when most seeds are exact ties, the median is pinned to 1 by the ties and says nothing about the seeds that did move. The last column counts how many pairs are within ±5% of parity.</p><table tabindex="0"><thead><tr><th style="text-align:right;">target</th><th style="text-align:right;">leaves</th><th style="text-align:right;">pairs</th><th style="text-align:right;">median ESS ratio [min, Q1, med, Q3, max]</th><th style="text-align:right;">within ±5%</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td style="text-align:right;"><code>eight_schools</code></td><td style="text-align:right;"><code>all_good_leaves</code></td><td style="text-align:right;">20</td><td style="text-align:right;">[0.980, 1.000, 1.000, 1.000, 1.663]</td><td style="text-align:right;">19 / 20</td></tr><tr><td style="text-align:right;"><code>eight_schools</code></td><td style="text-align:right;"><code>nuts_weighted</code></td><td style="text-align:right;">20</td><td style="text-align:right;">[1.000, 1.000, 1.000, 1.024, 1.505]</td><td style="text-align:right;">15 / 20</td></tr><tr><td style="text-align:right;"><code>funnel</code></td><td style="text-align:right;"><code>all_good_leaves</code></td><td style="text-align:right;">20</td><td style="text-align:right;">[1.000, 1.000, 1.000, 1.000, 1.000]</td><td style="text-align:right;">20 / 20</td></tr><tr><td style="text-align:right;"><code>funnel</code></td><td style="text-align:right;"><code>nuts_weighted</code></td><td style="text-align:right;">20</td><td style="text-align:right;">[1.000, 1.000, 1.000, 1.000, 1.000]</td><td style="text-align:right;">20 / 20</td></tr><tr><td style="text-align:right;"><code>radon_mn-radon_partially_pooled_centered</code></td><td style="text-align:right;"><code>all_good_leaves</code></td><td style="text-align:right;">20</td><td style="text-align:right;">[0.606, 0.830, 0.964, 1.174, 1.378]</td><td style="text-align:right;">3 / 20</td></tr><tr><td style="text-align:right;"><code>radon_mn-radon_partially_pooled_centered</code></td><td style="text-align:right;"><code>nuts_weighted</code></td><td style="text-align:right;">20</td><td style="text-align:right;">[0.663, 0.757, 1.003, 1.149, 1.525]</td><td style="text-align:right;">4 / 20</td></tr></tbody></table><h2 id="What-the-rows-conclude" tabindex="-1">What the rows conclude <a class="header-anchor" href="#What-the-rows-conclude" aria-label="Permalink to &quot;What the rows conclude {#What-the-rows-conclude}&quot;">​</a></h2><p>The sentence below is <strong>assembled from the numbers</strong> by <code>nw_conclusion</code>, not written here. It cannot disagree with the tables above, because it is computed from the same rows in the same build.</p><p>No universal winner: across the 4 discriminating (target, leaf-policy) cells, <code>stepsize</code> won paired minimum-coordinate efficiency in 1, <code>unit</code> in 1, and 2 were exact ties. Keep <code>unit</code> as the conservative default and retain <code>stepsize</code> as an experiment. That parity is an average, not a pattern: per-seed ratios on <code>eight_schools</code> / <code>nuts_weighted</code> span 0.73–2.2, and only 39 of 80 paired seeds land within ±5% of parity. The policies differ substantially on most seeds and merely disagree about which direction. The ties come from <code>eight_schools</code>, where the arms are bit-identical on at least half the seeds — so the paired median is pinned to exactly 1 by those seeds and measures nothing about the policies. Excluded outright as non-discriminating: <code>funnel</code> — every policy arm produced a bit-identical run there, so the ties they report are an artifact of the target rather than evidence that the policies are equivalent.</p><h2 id="Reproduce-it" tabindex="-1">Reproduce it <a class="header-anchor" href="#Reproduce-it" aria-label="Permalink to &quot;Reproduce it {#Reproduce-it}&quot;">​</a></h2><p>A re-run regenerates the rows and its own run-scoped configuration. It does not regenerate the fields describing the original measurement — <code>warmuphmc_sha</code>, <code>nonlinear_impl_sha</code>, <code>measured_on</code>, <code>measured_by</code>, <code>verification</code> — which is why those are recorded once, in the artifact, rather than restated here.</p><h2 id="See-also" tabindex="-1">See also <a class="header-anchor" href="#See-also" aria-label="Permalink to &quot;See also {#See-also}&quot;">​</a></h2><ul><li><p><a href="/WarmupHMC.jl/dev/linear-restart#Linear-restart-evidence">Linear restart evidence</a> — the same treatment for the linear path.</p></li><li><p><a href="/WarmupHMC.jl/dev/evidence#Benchmark-evidence">Benchmark evidence</a> — the appendix rendering every checked-in result file.</p></li></ul>',25)])])}const x=e(r,[["render",n]]);export{y as __pageData,x as default};
